/*
Definition for a Node.

class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;

    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteTail(ListNode head) {

        // Case 1: Empty list
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Case 2: Only one node
        if (head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Start from the head
        ListNode current = head;

        // Traverse until current reaches the last node
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        // current is now the tail
        // Move to the previous node
        current.prev.next = null;

        // Disconnect the deleted tail from the list
        current.prev = null;

        // Return the original head
        return head;
    }
}

Quick Book Revision

To delete the tail of a doubly linked list, handle the empty and single-node cases first. Traverse to the last node using current.next != null. Once at the tail, use current.prev.next = null to make the previous node the new tail, then set current.prev = null to disconnect the deleted node. Return the original head.

Time: O(n) — traverse to the tail.
Auxiliary Space: O(1) — only one pointer is used.
